WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
The smartest thing a high-performer can

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.920
do

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:12.360
is to hit the brakes.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.920
The best cars have powerful engines, but

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:17.640
they also have the best brakes to let

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:20.320
the drivers turn a corner without flying

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.320
off the track.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.680
The problem is that for driven people,

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:29.680
slowing down feels like failing. But the

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:31.840
same smarts that make you capable can

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.600
also get you stuck.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.600
Driven people suffer from the pressures

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:40.320
of perfectionism,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.680
being overwhelmed, and this existential

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.880
loneliness.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:48.560
Your instinct is usually to push harder.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:51.320
But pushing harder makes it even worse.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.720
What happens when your car is stuck in

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:57.720
the mud? You hit the gas,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:59.760
the tires spin, and you dig deeper.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:03.040
I've seen this over and over again at

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:05.440
MIT, on Wall Street, in boardrooms at

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:09.160
companies worth billions.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:11.960
So today, we will build four essential

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:14.040
mental brakes that stop your greatest

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:16.480
strengths from turning against you. And

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:19.240
the fourth is the hardest one for most

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:22.440
people to apply, but that's the one that

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.440
might matter the most.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:28.040
Let's go to the first brake. Being good

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:31.280
at thinking also mean you're very good

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:34.600
at overthinking. Overthinking is a loop

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.960
with no new input. The same thoughts go

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:39.440
on and on and on in circles, getting

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.840
louder with every cycle, and you don't

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.440
get anywhere. Overthinking solves

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:47.080
nothing. You can't think your way to

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:51.000
information you don't have.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:54.800
You end up spending 3 hours analyzing a

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:57.440
text message. Those dreadful three dots,

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:01.040
you You they appear, then they

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.720
disappear, and they appear again.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.360
Then silence.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:08.960
You replay and you reinterpret what they

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:12.360
said at dinner. You obsess over patterns

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.400
that may not exist. Imagining what

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:16.880
someone else thinks about you doesn't

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.680
make it accurate. Mind reading

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:22.800
is a terrible, terrible relationship

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.720
strategy. The less information you have,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:28.800
the more stories your brain creates.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:31.760
When information is missing, your inner

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:35.280
critic is happy to fill in the gaps.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:38.600
In a 2018 study, psychologist Erica

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:41.520
Boothby found that after a conversation,

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:44.600
people consistently underestimate how

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:47.400
much the other person liked them. No

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:50.920
amount of intelligence can manufacture

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:53.640
missing data. One honest, open

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.240
conversation can produce more useful

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.640
information

00:02:55.240 --> 00:03:00.959
and deeper connection than endless hours

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.800
of overthinking. So, when you catch

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:05.080
yourself circling, overtest your

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.880
assumption.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:09.200
First, ask

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:11.920
"What am I assuming?" What am I assuming

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:13.080
about this person, about this situation?

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.320
Second,

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:18.160
"What evidence will change my mind?"

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:21.320
That's a very important question. And

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:24.040
third, what is the smallest, honest way

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:27.519
to get to that evidence? Sometimes the

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:29.080
test is just four golden words,

00:03:27.519 --> 00:03:31.640
"What did you mean?"

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:33.080
Or "Can you tell me more?" Now, there's

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:35.600
one caveat.

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:39.080
Overtest doesn't mean keep testing

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:43.080
forever. Your goal is to not arrive at

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:46.519
100% certainty. You'll never get there.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:49.120
I apply the 70% rule. Most decisions

00:03:46.519 --> 00:03:51.320
should be made when you have about 70%

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:54.840
of the information you need. If you wait

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:56.040
for 90 or 95% you're probably moving too

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:59.000
slowly.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:01.560
At some point you have to stop asking

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.680
your brain for an answer it doesn't

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:04.600
have.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:06.880
Now let's go to the second break. For

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:09.560
those of us who are feeling

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:11.600
overcommitted, the strange reward for

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:13.720
being dependable

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:16.120
is that you have to carry more. People

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:18.280
keep asking you for more because you

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:20.239
make it seem so easy. Think about the

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:22.880
person in your family who keeps

00:04:20.239 --> 00:04:24.360
everything moving. In mine

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.400
it's my wife.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:30.440
School events, appointments, shopping,

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:32.360
there is no day off and she's also an

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:34.760
oncologist taking care of cancer

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.360
patients. She deals with life and death

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:39.320
issues at work.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:41.840
People with high self-control reported

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:45.760
feeling more burdened by how much their

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:49.200
co-workers and partners relied on them.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.560
In one study participants were given two

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.400
co-workers.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:55.000
One was highly disciplined or at least

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:57.120
they were told that. The other less so.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:59.280
Then the participants were asked to hand

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.480
out work to either one of them. Who

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:04.000
would you give the extra task to? They

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:06.440
gave more of it to the disciplined one

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.640
of course. And and that part is not

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:10.280
surprising.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:12.320
What came next was interesting. The

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:14.919
participants believed the disciplined

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.040
co-worker spent less effort getting the

00:05:14.919 --> 00:05:18.960
work done.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:21.880
The more capable you look the more

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:24.240
invisible your efforts become.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:27.200
And you know, I've made the same mistake

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.760
myself. When I was a CEO

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.400
we were very lucky. We were one of the

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:33.960
fastest growing companies in the US. I

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:37.160
would constantly tell myself, "Oh, it'll

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:39.520
be faster if I just do this myself and

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:42.280
oh, I'll just sleep on the plane."

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:45.120
But the cost of your commitments can

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:47.480
stay invisible even to you. So what What

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:51.640
you do when you feel over-committed? You

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:54.560
can overprice your yes. Make it rare.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:57.000
For every meaningful commitment, ask

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:58.760
three questions. What are the benefits

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:01.480
and who gets them?

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:03.960
What are the costs and who pays them?

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:06.840
These two questions help you triage. If

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:09.840
it helps everyone, keep it.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:12.840
If it helps you and costs others,

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:13.760
fix it. If it's helping no one, get rid

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:16.040
of it.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:18.919
But the dangerous one is when it

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:21.240
benefits others and you keep paying the

00:06:18.919 --> 00:06:24.480
costs. That's where the third question

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:28.320
comes in handy. Is this a gift

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:31.080
or tax? A gift can cost you enormously

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:32.960
and it might still be worth giving.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:34.919
Caring for your children, giving your

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:36.880
attention to your friend, those are

00:06:34.919 --> 00:06:38.880
gifts you can give willingly.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:42.520
Taxes on the other hand are the

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:45.000
commitments that are assumed, recurring,

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:47.520
and hard to renegotiate out of.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:49.160
Somewhere along the way, you stop

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.760
choosing it.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:53.440
That's where you increase the price of

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:55.640
your yes.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:57.720
At work, you can say, "Sure,

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:59.880
I can take this on.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:01.800
What comes off of my plate?"

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:04.200
With your friends, you can say, "Yeah,

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:06.280
I'll hold the book club if the rest of

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:09.440
you can handle the food."

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:11.840
You will keep carrying things for people

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.800
you love because some things in life

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:17.040
aren't transactions.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:19.280
But for everything else, name your price

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:21.120
before you say yes. Now comes the third

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.000
break. For those of us who suffer from

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.840
what I like to call perfectionitis, you

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.919
may tell yourself you are a

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:31.840
perfectionist, but all you have is a

00:07:28.919 --> 00:07:33.919
finish line that keeps moving. If done

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:36.040
means there's nothing left to improve,

00:07:33.919 --> 00:07:37.760
you'll never get there.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:39.440
But the version that you think isn't

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:42.920
good enough might be the version that

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:45.055
wins. A friend of mine is a gifted

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.080
musician, a phenomenal singer.

00:07:45.055 --> 00:07:47.520
>> [music]

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:50.960
>> For years she sang in other people's

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:54.960
bands, traveled the world, and she never

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:57.800
thought of herself as a songwriter. Then

00:07:54.960 --> 00:08:00.920
her son turned five. So she wrote an

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:03.800
entire album of children's songs

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:05.800
for him. And when it was finished, she

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:07.325
wasn't happy with it. It was like, "Ah,

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:07.360
these are simple songs."

00:08:07.325 --> 00:08:08.600
>> [music]

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:11.280
>> But then

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:14.640
she took a random chance and submitted

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:16.840
it to the Grammys.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:20.640
And that year, not only was she

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:22.640
nominated, she won the Grammy. It

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:24.720
completely changed her life.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:27.320
And I always think about that story. If

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:30.160
you keep over-polishing, you'll never

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:31.760
put your work out in the world. It makes

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:33.479
completion

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:36.320
emotionally

00:08:33.479 --> 00:08:38.800
impossible. A lot of what we think is

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:42.200
over-polishing is actually the fear of

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:45.120
rejection. I have it. For this channel,

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:46.800
I research and write everything you see,

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:49.640
everything you hear.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.960
And when I sit down to think about the

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:56.760
next topic, which is every week, it

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:58.920
nearly breaks me every single time

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:02.440
because I am convinced that I have

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:07.360
finally run out of ideas. And I sit at

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:10.440
my desk or go for a long walk convinced

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:11.960
that this is the week where it all comes

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:14.520
crashing down.

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:17.440
Goodbye YouTube, you know.

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:19.880
Then I remind myself that

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:20.880
all I have to do is to show up every

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:24.280
week.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:27.520
So I just start writing something. Maybe

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:29.200
it's a bad idea. Maybe it's a terrible

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:31.120
first draft.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:35.520
And I keep tinkering.

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:37.760
And every week, slowly but surely, that

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:39.520
little opening at the end of the tunnel,

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:41.600
it starts showing up.

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:45.480
And when I'm done with that video,

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:48.960
regardless of whether viewers like you

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:51.920
will receive it with love or not,

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:53.160
I only have one job to do come Monday

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:56.480
morning.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:58.640
Focus on shipping the next video. When

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:01.320
you want to over-polish,

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:04.480
try to over-ship. Tech companies call it

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:07.600
MVP, minimum viable product, the

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:10.480
smallest version that works in real

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:12.280
hands, so the next one can get better.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.520
You will always see something to

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:15.280
improve.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:16.520
That doesn't mean that you're not

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:18.120
finished.

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:19.960
And by the way, if you like these

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:23.200
videos, I think you'll like my

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:26.520
newsletter, too. I love writing it, one

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:28.920
insight, one tool, one practice, every

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:32.080
Tuesday. The link is below. It's totally

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:34.720
free. And now, the fourth break, because

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:38.320
if you are an over-committed

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:40.120
over-thinker who is an over-polisher,

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:41.880
it is very likely

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:44.040
that you are also feeling completely

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:45.760
alone.

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:48.800
I do sometimes.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:50.400
And that's why our last break is

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:53.160
over-ask.

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:56.240
The more capable you become, the easier

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:58.160
it is for people to assume you're fine.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:01.320
People can care about you deeply and

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:03.760
still have no idea what you need.

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:06.480
This is the most dangerous loop.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:09.480
You look fine and self-sustainable, so

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:12.040
nobody asks you if you're okay. So, you

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:14.640
decide to handle it all, and that makes

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:16.960
them believe that they should just leave

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:20.120
you alone, so they do,

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:23.400
and the spiral leaves you disconnected,

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:25.600
lonely, living a life only in your head.

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:28.360
I was having coffee in New York City

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:30.720
with a 28-year-old marketing manager,

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:33.960
and she told me she was worried that her

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.960
boss thought she was an underperformer

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:37.880
and she was planning on borrowing money

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:41.160
to go to business school and I asked

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:42.600
her, "How much of this are you actually

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:44.160
sure about?"

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:47.160
And she said she was sure about almost

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:48.920
none of it. She was afraid to ask

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:50.120
because she didn't want to hear the

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:53.160
answer.

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:55.960
And she was just all alone fighting her

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:57.640
own battles in her own head.

00:11:55.960 --> 00:12:00.800
So, we literally practice that

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:02.720
conversation at Starbucks for an hour.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:04.400
So, the next week she found the courage

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:06.080
and finally had that conversation with

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:08.000
her boss.

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:10.320
Now, there were some minor concerns

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:12.440
about her performance, but they loved

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:15.560
the way she was such a great culture

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:18.480
fit. There was no plan of firing her.

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:20.480
The conversation instead digressed into

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:22.920
what kind of training she should get

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:25.120
that the company would pay for. So, now

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:28.280
she can keep her job, she's not getting

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:29.520
fired, and can pursue her MBA all at the

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:31.520
same time.

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:34.000
All you have to do

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:37.280
is ask for help. Nobody can help with

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:39.600
something they can't see. Researchers at

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:42.720
University of Chicago found that people

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:44.360
who needed help guessed that others

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:46.800
won't give it

00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:49.600
or that it would be a burden.

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:52.560
They were wrong on both counts.

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:54.218
According to that study, people say yes

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:54.960
more often than you think.

00:12:54.218 --> 00:12:59.120
[clears throat]

00:12:54.960 --> 00:13:01.640
So, ask sooner, ask more often, ask

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:04.160
before it feels comfortable, ask before

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:06.400
you're drowning. One thing I've learned

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:10.720
is better to ask for something specific.

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:13.000
Can you look at this for 10 minutes or

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:15.960
"I've been feeling alone and cut off

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:18.360
lately. Could we take a walk in the city

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:21.800
next weekend? I'd love that."

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:24.720
And this is the final point. Give them

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:27.400
the permission to say no. That's okay.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:29.080
Shrug it off. Ask someone else.

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:31.440
And if they help,

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:32.760
tell them afterwards what their help

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:34.600
meant.

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:36.320
That's how asking becomes a

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:38.960
relationship.

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:40.240
Remember that question about gift versus

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:42.600
tax?

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:45.960
You're capable, right? So, you've spent

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:47.080
years giving gifts to everyone around

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:49.440
you.

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:50.720
When you ask your guides and your

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:53.080
partners

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:55.880
for help, remember

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:57.360
they're equally eager to give you that

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:59.960
same gift.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:03.880
You know, the tallest trees on Earth are

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:06.280
coast redwoods. Some rise more than 300

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:09.840
ft into the air,

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:12.000
but their roots don't go nearly as deep

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:14.440
as you might imagine.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:17.080
They spread outward

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:19.160
and wrap around the roots of the trees

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:21.640
next to them.

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:23.640
The tallest trees in the world don't

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:25.760
stand alone.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:28.160
You don't have to, either.

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:32.040
Let someone in.

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:34.600
Don't make the people who love you

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:36.360
guess how to love you.

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:39.160
Thank you.

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:39.160
And I love you.

